sources_check.out — figures re-read at their original source by Claude, 2026-10-09
(after three research passes; each line: source → verbatim text → where it is used).
PDF quotes were extracted with PyMuPDF from the arXiv PDFs; web quotes via fetch.

HARDWARE
- Apple Newsroom, 2026-03-04, "Say hello to MacBook Neo"
  https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/03/say-hello-to-macbook-neo/
  "8GB of unified memory"; "MacBook Neo starts at $599 (U.S.)"; footnote 1: "Bestselling
  PC laptop with the latest shipping Intel Core Ultra 5 processor" … "production Intel Core
  Ultra 5-based PC systems with Intel Graphics, 8GB of RAM, 256GB SSD".            → B01
- Microsoft, Windows 11 specifications (Copilot+ PC)
  https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/windows-11-specifications
  "an NPU capable of performing 40+ TOPS"; "16 GB DDR5/LPDDR5"; "256 GB SSD/UFS".   → B01
- Apple Newsroom, 2023-01-17, M2 Pro and M2 Max
  "200GB/s of unified memory bandwidth" (M2 Pro).                                   → B02, B04
- Gavia v0.3.1 release assets (gh api repos/nikhil-kunapareddy/gavia/releases):
  Gavia_0.3.1_aarch64.dmg, Gavia_0.3.1_x64-setup.exe, Gavia_0.3.1_amd64.AppImage,
  Gavia_0.3.1_amd64.deb, Gavia-0.3.1-1.x86_64.rpm — macOS, Windows, Linux.         → B01
- Gavia ROADMAP.md at 760e465, "Not planned": "Cloud processing or accounts. Gavia stays
  offline and on your computer." README.md: "It works without internet".           → B00

QUANTIZATION / SIZE
- Dettmers & Zettlemoyer, arXiv 2212.09720 (ICML 2023, PMLR 202:7750–7774), abstract:
  "We run more than 35,000 experiments with 16-bit inputs and k-bit parameters" … "at
  scales of 19M to 176B parameters" … "our findings show that {4-bit} precision is almost
  universally optimal for total model bits and zero-shot accuracy."                 → B03
- Huang et al., "An Empirical Study of LLaMA3 Quantization: From LLMs to MLLMs", arXiv
  2404.14047 (Visual Intelligence 2:36, 2024), Table 1 ("Evaluation results of post-training
  quantization on the LLaMA3-8B model"), WikiText2 PPL: FP16 6.1; GPTQ 4-bit g128 6.5;
  3-bit 8.2; 2-bit 2.1×10².                                                         → B03
- Apple, "Apple Intelligence Foundation Language Models", arXiv 2407.21075 (PDF):
  "a ∼3 billion parameter model designed to run efficiently on devices"; "compressed to only
  about 3.5 bits per weight (bpw) without significant quality loss. We choose to use 3.7 bpw
  in production".                                                                   → B03
- Gemini Team, arXiv 2312.11805 (PDF): "1.8B (Nano-1) and 3.25B (Nano-2) parameters,
  targeting low and high memory devices respectively" … "It is 4-bit quantized for
  deployment".                                                                      → B03
- Yuan et al., "LLM Inference Unveiled: Survey and Roofline Model Insights", arXiv
  2402.16363 (PDF): "in the decode stage, all computations are memory-bound, resulting in
  performance significantly below the computational capacity" of the GPU.           → B02

AGENTS
- OSWorld, Xie et al., arXiv 2404.07972 (NeurIPS 2024), abstract: "369 computer tasks";
  "While humans can accomplish over 72.36% of the tasks, the best model achieves only
  12.24% success".                                                                  → B06
- OSWorld-Verified results file — see evidence/osworld_check.out (best single model ≤9B:
  EvoCUA-8B 46.06%, 2026-01-13; the research pass's "AutoGLM-OS-9B 48.03%" is not in the
  verified file and is not used).                                                   → B06
- FaithEval, Ming et al., arXiv 2410.03727 ("published at ICLR 2025"), Table 4,
  "Performance comparison on Unanswerable Context", AVG strict: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct 0.376,
  Gemma-2-9b-it 0.503, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 0.290, Phi-3.5-mini 0.131, GPT-4o 0.597,
  Claude 3.5 Sonnet 0.621. "Abstaining is challenging, even when explicitly instructed." → B07
- Brynjolfsson, Li & Raymond, "Generative AI at Work", QJE 140(2):889–942 (2025),
  DOI 10.1093/qje/qjae044 (Crossref abstract): "data from 5,172 customer-support agents" …
  "increases worker productivity, as measured by issues resolved per hour, by 15% on
  average" … "the most experienced and highest-skilled workers see small gains in speed and
  small declines in quality". (The 2023 NBER version says 5,179 agents and 14%; the
  published figures are used.)                                                      → B07
- TinyAgent, Erdogan et al., arXiv 2409.00608 (EMNLP 2024 Demo), abstract: "fine-tune two
  small language models, TinyAgent-1.1B and 7B" … "can achieve, and even surpass, the
  function-calling capabilities of larger models like GPT-4-Turbo". Table figures (80.06%
  1.1B with ToolRAG vs GPT-4-Turbo 79.08%) from the research pass's reading of the PDF.  → B06
